# No magic numbers

Do not leave unexplained numeric literals in behavior code. Name values that encode domain meaning, UI layout, input protocols, timing, or rendering.

## Prefer

```ts
const MIDDLE_MOUSE_BUTTON = 1;
const VIEWPORT_SCALE_STEP = 0.1;
const DEFAULT_FONT_SIZE = 24;

if (event.button === MIDDLE_MOUSE_BUTTON) { /* … */ }
```

Colocate constants with the module that owns them, or share via a small `*-constants.ts` / existing domain module when reused across files.

## Allowed without naming

* Loop / array indices and obvious arithmetic (`i + 1`, `length - 1`, `/ 2` for midpoint).
* Identity math (`* 1`, `+ 0`) and empty defaults (`0`, `1` for scale/alpha when they are literal identities).
* Enum-like discriminants already expressed as string unions or typed enums.
* Test expectations that mirror a fixture’s explicit setup values in the same test.
* CSS in stylesheets / style objects when the number is a one-off layout token next to related styles (prefer design tokens when the value repeats).

## Do not

* Scatter the same literal (e.g. `0.1`, `24`, button `1`) across files — extract one shared constant.
* Name every trivial `0`/`1`; name values a reader would otherwise have to reverse-engineer.
* Put renderer/UI literals into serialized scene defaults unless they are real domain defaults (then name them in the domain/factory module).
